<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rabdentse</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rabdentse was the second capital of the kingdom of Sikkim, in western Sikkim near Pelling, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The second Chogyal, Tensung Namgyal, moved the court there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuksom">Yuksom</a> in 1670, and it remained the capital until 1793, when invasions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> forced a move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumlong">Tumlong</a>. The ruins of its palace and chortens are protected as a monument.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rabdentse.jpg" alt="Rabdentse" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rangan Datta Wiki · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rabdantse_6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The ruins stand on a ridge about 3 kilometres from upper Pelling in West Sikkim, with views of the Khanchendzonga ranges on one side and mountains and valleys on the other. They are reached from an ornamental yellow gate near the Pelling–Geyshing road, a walk of about 15 minutes past a lake and through forest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pemayangtse-monastery">Pemayangtse Monastery</a>, one of the oldest in Sikkim, stands close by, and the site lies on a Buddhist pilgrimage circuit that begins at the Dubdi Monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuksom">Yuksom</a> and includes Tashiding Monastery and Khecheopalri Lake.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The reign of Tensung Namgyal, who moved the court here, was peaceful. After his death in 1700 his young son Chador Namgyal was driven out by his half-sister Pendiongmu with help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a>, and spent ten years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, where he became state astrologer to the Sixth Dalai Lama, before returning with Tibetan support. Pendiongmu had him killed in 1716 by a Tibetan doctor's bloodletting at the Ralang hot spring; she and the doctor were then put to death.</p><p>His son Gurmed Namgyal, who succeeded in 1717, fortified Rabdentse against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkhas</a> and Bhutanese, and died in 1733 aged 26. Under Phuntsog Namgyal II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> made 17 invasions of Sikkim, and Tenzing Namgyal fled from Rabdentse to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, where he died in 1780. His son Tshudpud Namgyal, finding Rabdentse too close to the Nepalese border, moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumlong">Tumlong</a>, and the city was later reduced to ruins by the Nepalese army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>An avenue of chestnut trees leads to Namphogang, a throne of three standing stones from which judges gave their judgments, and to the half-ruined Taphap Chorten, where visitors to the palace dismounted and removed their hats. The palace had a northern wing for the royal family, with the ruined Dab Lhagang shrine, and a southern wing where the king received his subjects. Three chortens nearby are fairly well preserved, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> protects the site as a monument of national importance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rabdentse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later capitals included <b>Rabdentse</b> and Tumlong.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sikkim">Kingdom of Sikkim</a></li></ul></section>
